  "grid_title": "OUR RANKING",
  "why": "The order is our opinion; the facts are sourced. Swimming: the average competitive swimmer swims 60,000-80,000 m a week, about 30,000 rotations of each shoulder; shoulder pain is the most frequent musculoskeletal complaint in competitive swimmers, most often supraspinatus tendinopathy (Heinlein & Cosgarea 2010). Soccer: most ACL injuries happen without direct contact, from cutting, pivoting, sudden stops or bad landings (AAOS OrthoInfo). Gymnastics: elite female gymnasts landing a back somersault took ground reaction forces of 6.8-13.3 times body weight (Wade et al. 2012); spondylolysis is a stress fracture of the pars interarticularis, a thin part of the vertebra, linked to repeated back hyperextension, and 11 of 100 young female gymnasts had it in Jackson et al. 1976 (4 times their non-athletic peers). Climbing: the finger flexor pulleys (A2, A3, A4 most at risk) tear under the crimp grip; pulley injuries are the most common climbing injury and rare outside climbing (Schöffl 2015; Miro 2021). Pitching: an adult pitch puts about 64 N·m of valgus torque on the elbow, while the UCL fails at about 33 N·m, so every pitch approaches its limit (Langer 2006); UCL reconstruction (Tommy John surgery, first done by Dr. Frank Jobe on Tommy John in 1974) uses a tendon graft, and 25% of major league pitchers had had it in a 2015 survey (Conte 2015).",
